
Debug.Assert(LastIndexOf(Ret) == OLD(Ret)); // @@@ The return values of ArrayList should match the last appearance of the return value</code>

That is the assertion statement for the comment "// @@@ The return values of ArrayList should match the last appearance of the return value". The `Debug.Assert` is a built-in function in C# that checks the condition stated in the parentheses, and if the condition is not met, it breaks the execution, indicating an error. 

The `OLD(Ret)` keyword refers to the return value that the function held before it was called, and `Ret` is a reference to that return value. We are checking if the last value of the ArrayList matches its former return value. If any change occurs after the returned value is set, then an error will be raised by the `Debug.Assert`.

Similarly, for the line `int index = IndexOf(obj);`, we assert that the index obtained from running the `IndexOf` method is not less than 0. This makes sure that the object is in the list before removing it using the `RemoveAt` method:

